They said, Look, Jeff, you're calling

00:15:42.780 --> 00:15:46.410 position:61%
every day.
We don't have a preparatory job, but if

00:15:46.410 --> 00:15:51.450 position:63%
your work in the membership Development
Department, there's an opening is in the

00:15:51.450 --> 00:15:54.720 position:56%
ticket booth selling tickets and you
just.

00:15:54.900 --> 00:15:57.960
Would you work in the ticket booth?
I said, Absolutely.

00:15:58.320 --> 00:16:03.270 position:63%
And that's how I started at MoMA.
And then during breaks, I would start to

00:16:03.270 --> 00:16:06.990
promote their membership.
But how did you wind up selling

00:16:06.990 --> 00:16:10.650 position:59%
commodities at Smith Barney?
Because you did that for a while, too,

00:16:10.650 --> 00:16:13.320
right?
People would come up to me and they

00:16:13.320 --> 00:16:17.340 position:59%
would, you know, take out a membership
and they'd say, Jeff, you know, why

00:16:17.340 --> 00:16:21.480 position:63%
don't you come and work for me?
So I ended up thinking that for the type

00:16:21.480 --> 00:16:25.470 position:63%
of works that I wanted to make, I really
had to raise my income.

00:16:25.770 --> 00:16:30.570 position:56%
So I left the museum and I went into
becoming registered.

00:16:30.570 --> 00:16:35.820
I started off selling mutual funds.
I would go door to door, you know,

00:16:36.180 --> 00:16:37.920
basically.
I mean, I would call and make

00:16:37.920 --> 00:16:40.830 position:61%
appointments.
Then eventually I became registered for

00:16:40.830 --> 00:16:45.970 position:58%
commodities.
And, you know, being a broker, it's a

00:16:45.990 --> 00:16:49.770 position:63%
it's a salesperson.
I mean, I was trying to bring in people.

00:16:49.770 --> 00:16:53.300
I wasn't an analyst, but I was a
registered broker.

00:16:53.340 --> 00:16:57.200
So when you're doing this, you're
selling commodities and, you know,

00:16:57.210 --> 00:16:59.880 position:61%
you're trying to convince people to buy
open accounts and so forth.

00:17:00.090 --> 00:17:03.270 position:59%
Did you ever think I'm really going to
be a famous artist someday?

00:17:03.570 --> 00:17:06.450 position:59%
I can't say I was thinking about being
famous.

00:17:06.450 --> 00:17:11.250 position:63%
I wanted to participate.
You know, I wanted to give it everything

00:17:11.250 --> 00:17:14.670 position:63%
I had.
I always think about leading to the best

00:17:14.670 --> 00:17:17.550
of my ability.
And I would say yes to everything.

00:17:18.329 --> 00:17:21.780 position:56%
If somebody had asked me to be in an
exhibition, I would tell them yes.

00:17:21.780 --> 00:17:26.550 position:63%
And I would try to participate, make the
best work that I could.

00:17:27.520 --> 00:17:31.300 position:56%
I was ambitious.
I had tremendous respect for artists

00:17:31.300 --> 00:17:36.220 position:56%
like Warhol and Lichtenstein, but my
ambition wasn't the fame.

00:17:36.730 --> 00:17:42.550
It was to be part of a group
the power, the power of art, the

00:17:42.910 --> 00:17:48.610 position:63%
transcending power of becoming a person.
I want to become the person I could

00:17:48.610 --> 00:17:51.850 position:61%
become.
When did you say, okay, I'm good enough

00:17:51.850 --> 00:17:55.990 position:61%
as an artist to sell some art and I
don't need to sell commodities anymore?

00:17:56.020 --> 00:18:02.260 position:63%
I reached a point where I couldn't think
about anything other than my work, so I,

00:18:02.440 --> 00:18:09.820 position:63%
you know, should be trying to, you know,
open some more accounts, be able to give

00:18:09.820 --> 00:18:14.470 position:63%
more service to people.
But instead, I'd be on the phone talking

00:18:14.470 --> 00:18:18.040
to Dr.
Richard Feynman and talking about

00:18:18.040 --> 00:18:21.550 position:61%
equilibrium and how can I get permanent
equilibrium?

00:18:21.730 --> 00:18:25.000 position:59%
Can I do it really within the confines
of an aquarium?

00:18:25.510 --> 00:18:27.790
And I couldn't think about anything
else.

00:18:28.270 --> 00:18:35.440 position:61%
And so I was accepting, you know, all
the ramifications that if I made a show

00:18:35.440 --> 00:18:39.910 position:56%
and it wasn't successful, that, you
know, maybe I have to go pump gas, I

00:18:39.910 --> 00:18:43.960
have to start all over.
But I didn't have any options.

00:18:43.990 --> 00:18:46.750 position:56%
Richard Feynman, for those who don't
know, is one of the most famous

00:18:46.750 --> 00:18:51.130 position:56%
physicists of the 20th century Nobel
Prize winner, among other things.

00:18:51.490 --> 00:18:56.680
How did you get him on the phone?
I called him, and so I was in an

00:18:56.680 --> 00:18:59.410 position:61%
airplane flying back, I think, from Los
Angeles.

00:18:59.740 --> 00:19:02.950 position:61%
And I saw that he had just released the
book.

00:19:03.760 --> 00:19:05.770
Surely you're joking, Dr.
Feynman.

00:19:06.130 --> 00:19:12.310 position:63%
And I thought, oh, my gosh, this I was
reading that he loves to play bongos and

00:19:12.610 --> 00:19:14.860
he likes art.
He paints.

00:19:15.220 --> 00:19:21.670 position:63%
So after, you know, a day when I landed,
I called up Feynman and I told him the

00:19:21.670 --> 00:19:25.150
work that I wanted to create.
I want to create these equilibrium

00:19:25.150 --> 00:19:28.390 position:63%
tanks.
But I was worried that I didn't know how

00:19:28.390 --> 00:19:32.710 position:61%
to create permanent equilibrium in such
a confined space.

00:19:33.010 --> 00:19:36.820 position:61%
And he told me I could do it.
So were you looking for new things that

00:19:36.820 --> 00:19:40.870
you haven't conquered yet?
I've always loved philosophy, and I

00:19:40.870 --> 00:19:45.160 position:59%
think one of the reasons I work with a
reflective surface is because in

00:19:45.160 --> 00:19:50.590 position:63%
philosophy, the word you notice the most
is to reflect and reflection.

00:19:50.860 --> 00:19:54.550
I want to continue to reflect.
I want to continue to be calm.

00:19:55.450 --> 00:20:00.640 position:56%
You know, the art happens inside us.
These objects aren't art.

00:20:01.420 --> 00:20:05.290
They're just paintings.
They're just forms.

00:20:05.710 --> 00:20:11.170 position:63%
But art is really the essence that we
can feel as people of our own potential.

00:20:11.650 --> 00:20:15.280 position:59%
So if somebody is watching and says, I
want to be the next Jeff Koons, what

00:20:15.280 --> 00:20:19.840 position:58%
advice would you give to that person?
I would tell them to trust in

00:20:19.840 --> 00:20:23.320 position:63%
themselves.
After you trust in yourself, you want to

00:20:23.320 --> 00:20:27.520
go outside yourself.
And if you really focus on your

00:20:27.520 --> 00:20:31.930
interests, it connects you to a
universal vocabulary.

00:20:33.100 --> 00:20:36.070 line:92%
You.

00:20:43.020 --> 00:20:47.910 position:58%
So what is it that you think makes an
artist who goes from being a not well

00:20:47.910 --> 00:20:51.210 position:61%
known, maybe struggling artist to being
a famous artist?

00:20:51.540 --> 00:20:57.480 position:58%
I think my family gave me a lot of
support, emotional support when I was

00:20:57.480 --> 00:21:01.380 position:59%
younger.
I can't say that I was a loner growing

00:21:01.380 --> 00:21:05.760
up, but, you know, I was thinking
philosophically and my mind was

00:21:05.760 --> 00:21:10.080 position:63%
wondering about what the possibilities I
could find in life.

00:21:10.500 --> 00:21:15.840 position:63%
But to be able to be part of a group was
really meaningful to me, and the art

00:21:15.840 --> 00:21:19.650 position:59%
world represented that.
So if somebody is watching and says, I

00:21:19.650 --> 00:21:22.680 position:59%
want to be the next Jeff Koons, I want
to be a famous artist.

00:21:23.040 --> 00:21:25.410
What advice would you give to that
person?

00:21:25.800 --> 00:21:32.130 position:63%
I would tell them to trust in themselves
and to focus on your interests.

00:21:32.520 --> 00:21:39.630 position:61%
And what I found in my own life that
when I really focus on my interests and

00:21:39.630 --> 00:21:43.350 position:56%
there was a certain point where I
learned how to trust in myself, when

00:21:43.830 --> 00:21:47.730 position:63%
after you trust in yourself, you want to
go outside yourself.

00:21:48.300 --> 00:21:52.830
And if you really focus on your
interests, it connects you to a

00:21:52.830 --> 00:21:57.180 position:59%
universal vocabulary.
Now, did your parents live to see your

00:21:57.180 --> 00:22:00.700
success as a famous artist?
They did.

00:22:00.720 --> 00:22:06.090 position:59%
And, you know, I'm very grateful that
they were able to see that the type of

00:22:06.090 --> 00:22:10.320 position:61%
support that they gave to me to be able
to believe in myself.

00:22:10.890 --> 00:22:17.520
They brought me up also to be
self-reliant, that I was able to at

00:22:17.520 --> 00:22:20.160 position:61%
least accomplish part of my dreams that
they saw.

00:22:20.520 --> 00:22:25.020 position:59%
So a new world you want to conquer are
just continuing to find new ways to

00:22:25.020 --> 00:22:27.780 position:56%
express yourself in art.
And you're sounds like you're a very

00:22:27.780 --> 00:22:31.380
happy person.
I want to be, you know, a greater

00:22:31.530 --> 00:22:33.720 position:56%
person.
I won't be the best person I can be.

00:22:33.720 --> 00:22:37.770 position:59%
I want to be the best artist I can be.
The role models for me.

00:22:37.950 --> 00:22:42.090
Artists like Titian and Picasso,
Matisse.

00:22:42.090 --> 00:22:45.920 position:61%
These artists got better and better the
older they got.

00:22:45.930 --> 00:22:49.140
I mean, late Titian,
you know, it's.

00:22:49.860 --> 00:22:53.070 position:59%
It's incredible.
Not that you're going anywhere anytime

00:22:53.070 --> 00:22:55.650
soon, but what would you like your
legacy to be?

00:22:55.680 --> 00:23:01.380 position:58%
David I would hope that I could be
somewhat of a role model that if I've

00:23:01.380 --> 00:23:08.160 position:63%
been able to accomplish what I have been
able to, that anybody feels like they

00:23:08.160 --> 00:23:11.580 position:56%
can do it too.
That if they have the interests that

00:23:11.580 --> 00:23:16.260 position:59%
they would really like to participate,
be involved in the dialog of art.

00:23:16.290 --> 00:23:21.210 position:63%
You know, art's so amazing.
I mean, I was this kid from Pennsylvania

00:23:21.660 --> 00:23:25.380 position:58%
who went to art school.
But art connects you to all the human

00:23:25.380 --> 00:23:28.530
disciplines.
Art is not this hierarchy.

00:23:28.530 --> 00:23:33.870 position:61%
The whole idea of a hierarchy that you
need to be informed about something, it

00:23:33.870 --> 00:23:37.650 position:56%
doesn't exist.
People do that for their own kind of

00:23:37.650 --> 00:23:41.040
empowerment to disempower you, the
viewer.

00:23:41.400 --> 00:23:48.510 position:58%
You don't need anything in art other
than your own past experience to that

00:23:48.510 --> 00:23:51.540 position:58%
moment.
And I hope that I'm a symbol of that.

00:23:51.910 --> 00:23:55.470 line:92%
Who?

